Course.start({"num":29,"unit":6,"slug":"m29-parabolas","minutes":40,"calculator":"allowed","title":"Quadratic Functions and Parabolas","subtitle":"Graphs of y = ax² + bx + c: direction, vertex, axis of symmetry, intercepts and real-world arcs","lede":"Throw a ball, shoot a basketball, spray a water fountain: the path is a curve called a parabola. This week you'll graph quadratic functions and read their most important features.","recap":"√ undoes squaring. Simplify radicals with the largest perfect square factor: √50 = 5√2. In a right triangle, a² + b² = c².","goals":["Graph a quadratic function from a table of values","Tell whether a parabola opens up or down, and find its y-intercept","Find the vertex and axis of symmetry, and read zeros from a graph","Interpret a quadratic model of a thrown object"],"context":{"title":"Galileo's rolling balls","place":"Padua, Italy · about 1608","text":"For 2,000 years, people believed what the Greek philosopher **Aristotle** taught: a thrown object flies in a straight line until its \"push\" runs out, then falls straight down. It sounds reasonable. It's wrong.\n\nAround **1608**, **Galileo Galilei**, a professor at the University of Padua, tested it. He rolled bronze balls down a slanted ramp on a table, let them fly off the edge, and marked exactly where they landed on the floor. His notes still exist (a page called *folio 116v*), full of measurements.\n\nThe results showed that a launched ball's path is a smooth curve, a **parabola**. The horizontal motion stays steady, while the falling distance grows with the **square** of the time. Galileo published the full explanation in 1638 in *Two New Sciences*, written while he was under house arrest for his astronomy.\n\nEvery thrown ball, every jump, every arc of water from a fountain follows a parabola (if air resistance is small). The equation behind it has an {{x^2}} in it: a **quadratic function**.","ask":"When you throw a ball straight up, it slows down, stops for an instant at the top, then speeds up coming down. Where on the path is that \"stop\"?"},"learn":[{"title":"The shape of y = ax² + bx + c","text":"A **quadratic function** has the form {{y = ax^2 + bx + c}} (with {{a != 0}}). Its graph is a **parabola**, a U shape.\n\n- If {{a > 0}}, it **opens up** (like a smile) and has a **minimum** point.\n- If {{a < 0}}, it **opens down** (like a frown) and has a **maximum** point.\n- The **y-intercept** is {{c}} (put x = 0).\n\nThe simplest one is {{y = x^2}}: points (−2, 4), (−1, 1), (0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 4).","figs":["up","down"],"after":"Left: {{y = x^2 - 4x + 3}} opens up. Right: {{y = -x^2 + 2x + 3}} opens down."},{"title":"Vertex, axis of symmetry and zeros","text":"- The **vertex** is the turning point: the lowest point (if it opens up) or highest (if down).\n- The **axis of symmetry** is the vertical line through the vertex. The parabola is a mirror image on each side. Its equation is\n\n> {{x = -b/(2a)}}\n\n- The **zeros** (or **x-intercepts**, or **roots**) are where the graph crosses the x-axis, where {{y = 0}}. A parabola can have 2, 1 or 0 of them.","worked":[{"problem":"Find the vertex of {{y = x^2 - 4x + 3}}.","steps":[["{{a = 1}}, {{b = -4}}","Read a and b."],["{{x = -(-4)/(2 * 1) = 2}}","Axis of symmetry: x = 2."],["{{y = 2^2 - 4(2) + 3 = -1}}","Plug x = 2 back in."],["Vertex: {{(2, -1)}}","It opens up (a > 0), so this is the minimum."]]}],"check":{"q":"Does {{y = -3x^2 + x + 5}} open up or down, and what's its y-intercept?","choices":[["down; 5",true,"a = −3 is negative, and c = 5."],["up; 5",false,"Look at the sign of a."],["down; −3",false,"The y-intercept is c, not a."]]}},{"title":"Parabolas in motion","text":"For an object thrown or launched upward (in feet and seconds), the height {{h}} after {{t}} seconds is\n\n> {{h = -16t^2 + v t + s}}\n\nwhere {{v}} is the starting upward speed (ft/s) and {{s}} is the starting height. The −16 comes from gravity, and it's negative, so the parabola opens **down**: up, then back down.\n\n- The **vertex** gives the **maximum height** and when it happens.\n- The positive **zero** is when it **hits the ground**.","worked":[{"problem":"A ball is thrown up from 6 ft at 32 ft/s: {{h = -16t^2 + 32t + 6}}. When does it reach its highest point, and how high?","steps":[["{{t = -32/(2(-16)) = 1}} second","Vertex: t = −b/(2a)."],["{{h = -16(1)^2 + 32(1) + 6 = 22}} feet"]]}]}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"select","title":"Read the equation","items":["{{y = 2x^2 - 3x + 1}} opens {up|down}.","{{y = -x^2 + 6x}} opens {down|up} and has a {maximum|minimum}.","The y-intercept of {{y = x^2 + 4x - 7}} is {−7|4|1}.","The axis of symmetry of {{y = x^2 - 6x + 2}} is {x = 3|x = −3|x = 6}.","{{y = 5 - x^2}} opens {down|up}."],"hints":["The sign of a (the number with x²) tells up or down.","Axis of symmetry: x = −b/(2a)."]},{"id":"t2","type":"grid","title":"Table of values","intro":"Complete the table for {{y = x^2 - 4x + 3}}. Notice the symmetry.","tables":[{"corner":"{{x}}","cols":["{{y = x^2 - 4x + 3}}"],"fn":"x^2 - 4x + 3","rows":[["0","3"],["1","0"],["2","-1"],["3","0"],["4","3"],["5","8"],["-1","8"]],"given":[[0,0]]}],"hints":["For x = −1: {{(-1)^2 - 4(-1) + 3 = 1 + 4 + 3}}.","The y values repeat around the vertex at x = 2."]},{"id":"t3","type":"plot","title":"Graph the parabola","intro":"Make a table on paper first. Use x values on both sides of the vertex.","items":[{"q":"Plot **5 points** on {{y = x^2 - 4x + 3}} (use your table).","fn":"x^2 - 4x + 3","need":5,"curve":true,"x":[-3,7],"y":[-3,9]},{"q":"Plot **5 points** on {{y = -x^2 + 4}}.","fn":"-x^2 + 4","need":5,"curve":true}],"hints":["Find the vertex first, then go 1 and 2 steps to each side.","Points on each side of the vertex have the same y."]},{"id":"t4","type":"solve","title":"Find the vertex","pick":4,"intro":"Type the vertex as an ordered pair, like **(2, -1)**.","items":[{"q":"{{y = x^2 - 6x + 5}}","a":"(3, -4)","how":["{{x = 6/2 = 3}}","{{y = 9 - 18 + 5 = -4}}"]},{"q":"{{y = x^2 + 2x - 3}}","a":"(-1, -4)","how":["{{x = -2/2 = -1}}","{{y = 1 - 2 - 3 = -4}}"]},{"q":"{{y = -x^2 + 4x + 1}}","a":"(2, 5)","how":["{{x = -4/(-2) = 2}}","{{y = -4 + 8 + 1 = 5}}"]},{"q":"{{y = 2x^2 - 8x + 7}}","a":"(2, -1)","how":["{{x = 8/4 = 2}}","{{y = 8 - 16 + 7 = -1}}"]},{"q":"{{y = x^2 - 9}}","a":"(0, -9)","how":["b = 0, so x = 0","{{y = -9}}"]},{"q":"{{y = -3x^2 + 6x}}","a":"(1, 3)","how":["{{x = -6/(-6) = 1}}","{{y = -3 + 6 = 3}}"]}],"hints":["x = −b/(2a). Be careful with signs.","Then substitute that x to find y."]},{"id":"t5","type":"quiz","title":"Read the graph","fig":"read","intro":"Use the graph of {{y = x^2 - 2x - 3}}.","questions":[{"q":"What are the zeros (x-intercepts)?","choices":[["{{x = -1}} and {{x = 3}}",true],["{{x = -3}} only",false,"That's the y-intercept's value, on the y-axis."],["{{x = 1}}",false,"That's the axis of symmetry."]]},{"q":"What's the vertex?","choices":[["{{(1, -4)}}",true],["{{(0, -3)}}",false,"That's the y-intercept."],["{{(-4, 1)}}",false,"x first: 1 right, 4 down."]]},{"q":"What's the axis of symmetry?","choices":[["{{x = 1}}",true],["{{y = 1}}",false,"The axis is a vertical line: x = …"],["{{x = -4}}",false]]},{"q":"What's the minimum value of y?","choices":[["{{-4}}",true],["{{1}}",false],["{{-3}}",false]]}]},{"id":"t6","type":"read","title":"The basketball shot","intro":"Calculator allowed. A basketball is shot from a height of **6 ft** with an upward speed of **24 ft/s**: {{h = -16t^2 + 24t + 6}}.","fig":"ball","questions":[{"q":"When does it reach its maximum height?","choices":[["{{t = 0.75}} s",true,"{{t = -24/(2(-16)) = 0.75}}"],["{{t = 1.5}} s",false],["{{t = 24}} s",false]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"What's the maximum height?","choices":[["15 ft",true,"{{-16(0.75)^2 + 24(0.75) + 6 = -9 + 18 + 6 = 15}}"],["6 ft",false],["24 ft",false]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"What does the **6** in the equation mean?","choices":[["the ball starts 6 ft above the ground",true],["it takes 6 seconds",false],["the maximum height is 6 ft",false]]},{"q":"The hoop is 10 ft high. Is the ball ever at 10 ft?","choices":[["Yes, twice: going up and coming down",true,"It goes higher than 10 (up to 15), so it passes 10 ft on the way up and again on the way down."],["No, never",false],["Only once, at the top",false]]}]}],"build":{"heading":"Describe a parabola","prompt":"Choose a quadratic function: {{y = x^2 - 2x - 8}}, or make your own. Describe its graph completely: does it open **up or down**, what's its **y-intercept**, its **axis of symmetry**, its **vertex**, and (if you can find them from a table) its **zeros**? Show how you found the vertex.","model":"I chose y = x^2 - 2x - 8. It opens up because a = 1 is positive. The y-intercept is -8. The axis of symmetry is x = -(-2)/(2·1) = 1. The vertex is (1, -9), because 1 - 2 - 8 = -9. From a table, y = 0 when x = -2 and x = 4, so those are the zeros.","placeholder":"I chose y = …","reqs":[{"t":"Say whether it opens *up* or *down*","re":"\\bopens\\s+(up|down)"},{"t":"Give the axis of symmetry (x = …)","re":"\\b(axis|symmetry)\\b"},{"t":"Give the vertex as an ordered pair","re":"vertex[^.]*\\(\\s*[-−]?\\d+\\.?\\d*\\s*,\\s*[-−]?\\d+\\.?\\d*\\s*\\)"},{"t":"Mention the y-intercept","re":"\\by[- ]intercept\\b"}]},"reflect":{"q":"Galileo tested an idea everyone had believed for 2,000 years, and found it was wrong. Is there something \"everyone knows\" that you've questioned? How would you test it?","placeholder":"Something people believe that I'd like to test is…"},"challenge":[{"id":"x1","type":"solve","title":"Vertex form","intro":"In **vertex form**, {{y = a(x - h)^2 + k}}, the vertex is {{(h, k)}}.","items":[{"q":"Vertex of {{y = (x - 3)^2 + 1}}","a":"(3, 1)","how":["h = 3, k = 1"]},{"q":"Vertex of {{y = -2(x + 4)^2 - 5}}","a":"(-4, -5)","how":["{{x + 4 = x - (-4)}}, so h = −4; k = −5"]},{"q":"Write {{y = (x - 1)^2 - 4}} in standard form.","a":"y = x^2 - 2x - 3","how":["{{(x - 1)^2 = x^2 - 2x + 1}}","{{x^2 - 2x + 1 - 4 = x^2 - 2x - 3}}"]},{"q":"Find the vertex of {{y = -2x^2 + 8x - 3}}.","a":"(2, 5)","how":["{{x = -8/(-4) = 2}}","{{y = -8 + 16 - 3 = 5}}"]}]},{"id":"x2","type":"solve","title":"Launch problems","intro":"Use {{h = -16t^2 + vt + s}}.","items":[{"q":"A rocket is launched from the ground at **96 ft/s**. What's its maximum height?","a":"144","post":"feet","how":["{{h = -16t^2 + 96t}}","Vertex at {{t = 3}}: {{h = -144 + 288 = 144}} ft"]},{"q":"Same rocket: when does it land? ({{-16t^2 + 96t = 0}})","a":"6","post":"seconds","how":["{{-16t(t - 6) = 0}}","t = 0 (launch) or **t = 6**"]}]}],"figs":{"up":{"x":[-2,6],"y":[-3,8],"w":200,"lines":[{"fn":"x^2 - 4x + 3"}],"points":[[2,-1,"vertex"]],"alt":"Parabola opening up with vertex (2, −1)"},"down":{"x":[-3,5],"y":[-3,6],"w":200,"lines":[{"fn":"-x^2 + 2x + 3","c":1}],"points":[[1,4,"vertex",1]],"alt":"Parabola opening down with vertex (1, 4)"},"read":{"x":[-3,5],"y":[-5,6],"w":260,"lines":[{"fn":"x^2 - 2x - 3"},{"xeq":1,"c":2,"dash":true}],"points":[[-1,0],[3,0],[1,-4],[0,-3,"",1]],"alt":"Parabola crossing the x-axis at −1 and 3, vertex (1, −4), axis x = 1 dashed"},"ball":{"x":[0,2],"y":[0,16],"xstep":0.25,"ystep":2,"w":280,"h":240,"xevery":2,"xlabel":"seconds","ylabel":"feet","lines":[{"fn":"-16x^2 + 24x + 6","dom":[0,1.73]},{"fn":"10","c":2,"dash":true,"label":"hoop height"}],"points":[[0.75,15,"(0.75, 15)"]],"alt":"Arc of the basketball, highest at (0.75, 15), with a dashed line at 10 feet"}},"culture":"**Did you know?** Satellite dishes and car headlights are shaped like parabolas spun around their axis. A parabola reflects every incoming straight beam to one point, the focus, which is why a dish can gather a weak signal from space.","unitTitle":"Polynomials and Quadratics","code":"ODM2MS1PRUxJTEFH","star":"MTczMy1TSUdBTQ==","total":30,"prev":"m28-square-roots.html","next":"m30-solving-quadratics.html"});
